## [Providing data products on AWS Marketplace](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/data-exchange/latest/userguide/providing-data-sets.md)

- [Getting started as a provider](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/data-exchange/latest/userguide/provider-getting-started.md): Learn the process for becoming a data product provider on AWS Data Exchange.
- [Publishing guidelines](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/data-exchange/latest/userguide/publishing-guidelines.md): Guidelines for publishing products on AWS Data Exchange.

### [Publishing a new product](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/data-exchange/latest/userguide/publishing-products.md)

Learn how to publish products on AWS Data Exchange.

- [Containing file-based data](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/data-exchange/latest/userguide/publish-data-product.md): The following topics describe the process of creating a data set and publishing a new product in AWS Data Exchange containing file-based data on AWS Data Exchange by using the AWS Data Exchange console.
- [Containing APIs](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/data-exchange/latest/userguide/publish-API-product.md): The following topics describe the process of creating a REST API data set and publishing a new product that contains APIs on AWS Data Exchange.
- [Containing Amazon Redshift data sets](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/data-exchange/latest/userguide/publish-Redshift-product.md): An Amazon Redshift data set contains AWS Data Exchange datashares for Amazon Redshift.
- [Containing Amazon S3 data access](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/data-exchange/latest/userguide/publish-s3-data-access-product.md): With AWS Data Exchange for Amazon S3, providers can share direct access to Amazon S3 buckets or specific prefixes and Amazon S3 objects.
- [Containing AWS Lake Formation data permission data sets (Preview)](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/data-exchange/latest/userguide/publish-LF-data-product.md): If you're interested in publishing products containing AWS Lake Formation data permission data sets during this Preview, contact AWS Support.

### [Product best practices](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/data-exchange/latest/userguide/product-details.md)

Understand conceptual information about providing product details in AWS Data Exchange.

- [Product visibility](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/data-exchange/latest/userguide/product-visibility.md): New products initially have limited visibility, accessible only to allowlisted accounts and the product creator.
- [Sensitive categories of information](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/data-exchange/latest/userguide/sensitive-information.md): When you create a product in AWS Data Exchange, you must specify whether your product contains any personal data or sensitive categories of information.
- [Product details](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/data-exchange/latest/userguide/prod-details-over.md): The following topics provide best practices for the details of a product in AWS Data Exchange.
- [Revision access rules](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/data-exchange/latest/userguide/best-practices-revisions.md): Revision access rules specify which revisions subscribers can access when they subscribe to your product in AWS Data Exchange.
- [Data dictionaries](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/data-exchange/latest/userguide/data-dictionaries-pro.md): A data dictionary is a visual representation of the contents of your data set in AWS Data Exchange.
- [Samples](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/data-exchange/latest/userguide/samples-pro.md): A sample is a small part of the data in your product on AWS Data Exchange that is intended to show what the entire data set is like.

### [Product description templates](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/data-exchange/latest/userguide/product-description-templates.md)

Learn about the product description templates available for your use in AWS Data Exchange.

- [Generic template](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/data-exchange/latest/userguide/generic-template.md): When listing a product on AWS Data Exchange, you should include a long description that contains all the information necessary for subscribers to understand what your product offers.
- [Financial services template](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/data-exchange/latest/userguide/financial-template.md): When listing a product on AWS Data Exchange, you should include a long description that contains all the information necessary for subscribers to understand what your product offers.
- [Healthcare and life sciences template](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/data-exchange/latest/userguide/healthcare-template.md): When listing a product on AWS Data Exchange, you should include a long description that contains all the information necessary for subscribers to understand what your product offers.
- [Marketing and advertising template](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/data-exchange/latest/userguide/marketing-advertising-template.md): When listing a product on AWS Data Exchange, you should include a long description that contains all the information necessary for subscribers to understand what your product offers.
- [Media and entertainment template](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/data-exchange/latest/userguide/media-entertainment-template.md): When listing a product on AWS Data Exchange, you should include a long description that contains all the information necessary for subscribers to understand what your product offers.
- [Public sector template](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/data-exchange/latest/userguide/public-sector-template.md): When listing a product on AWS Data Exchange, you should include a long description that contains all the information necessary for subscribers to understand what your product offers.
- [Retail and location template](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/data-exchange/latest/userguide/retail-location-template.md): When listing a product on AWS Data Exchange, you should include a long description that contains all the information necessary for subscribers to understand what your product offers.

### [Creating offers](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/data-exchange/latest/userguide/prepare-offers.md)

Learn about the key points for creating offers for products in AWS Data Exchange.

- [Private offers](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/data-exchange/latest/userguide/private-offer-configuration.md): Describes how to create private offers for your product.
- [Public offers](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/data-exchange/latest/userguide/public-offer-configuration.md): Describes how to create public offers for your product.
- [BYOS offers](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/data-exchange/latest/userguide/create-byos-offers.md): Learn how to create bring your own subscription offers.
- [Viewing subscriptions](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/data-exchange/latest/userguide/view-subscriptions-for-product.md): You can view all of the subscriptions for any of your products through the Product overview page.

### [Updating data products](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/data-exchange/latest/userguide/updating-products.md)

How to update products in AWS Marketplace Management Portal.

- [Updating product and offer details](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/data-exchange/latest/userguide/update-product-details.md): After you publish a product, you can use the AWS Data Exchange console to edit the product details.
- [Updating a data dictionary](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/data-exchange/latest/userguide/update-data-dictionary.md): You can update a data dictionary in AWS Marketplace Management Portal by first removing the existing data dictionary and then uploading a new one.
- [Updating a sample](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/data-exchange/latest/userguide/update-sample.md): After you publish a product, you can update the samples in AWS Marketplace Management Portal.
- [Publishing a new data set revision](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/data-exchange/latest/userguide/dynamically-updated-products.md): AWS Data Exchange supports dynamically updated products.
- [Unpublish a product](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/data-exchange/latest/userguide/unpublish-product.md): After your product is published in AWS Data Exchange, it's available for all to find and subscribe to, based on the product's visibility settings.
- [Removing a revision](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/data-exchange/latest/userguide/remove-revision.md): A provider can revoke subscriber access to a revision and then delete the assets of the revision using the console or the AWS Data Exchange API.

### [Revoking revisions](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/data-exchange/latest/userguide/revoking-revisions.md)

As a provider of data products in AWS Data Exchange, you can revoke subscriber access to a specific revision at any time.

- [Using AWS CLI](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/data-exchange/latest/userguide/revoke-rev-sdk.md): As a provider of AWS Data Exchange data products, you can use the AWS CLI to revoke subscriber access to a revision using the following instructions.
- [Using the console for single revision (Provider)](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/data-exchange/latest/userguide/revoke-rev-single.md): As a provider of AWS Data Exchange data products, you can use the AWS Data Exchange console to revoke subscriber access to a single revision using the following instructions.
- [Using the console for multiple revisions (Provider)](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/data-exchange/latest/userguide/revoke-rev-multi.md): As a provider of AWS Data Exchange data products, you can use the AWS Data Exchange console to revoke subscriber access to multiple revisions using the following instructions.
- [Editing a revocation reason (Provider)](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/data-exchange/latest/userguide/edit-revoked-rev.md): As a provider of AWS Data Exchange data products, you can use the AWS Data Exchange console to edit the reason for the revocation using the following instructions.
- [Viewing revoked revisions (Subscriber)](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/data-exchange/latest/userguide/view-revoked-rev.md): As a subscriber to AWS Data Exchange data products, you can use the AWS Data Exchange console to view the reason for revocation of access to a revision using the following instructions.

### [Subscription verification for providers](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/data-exchange/latest/userguide/subscription-verification-pro.md)

Guidance for subscription verification for providers.

- [Email notifications](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/data-exchange/latest/userguide/email-notifications-pro.md): You will receive an email message to your AWS Marketplace registered Seller AWS account email address to notify you when an AWS Data Exchange subscription request is received, or when its status has changed to cancelled or expired.
- [Viewing subscription verification requests](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/data-exchange/latest/userguide/view-requests.md): After you publish a public offer and receive subscription verification requests, you can view the requests.
- [Approve or decline requests](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/data-exchange/latest/userguide/approve-or-decline-requests.md)
- [Provider-generated notifications](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/data-exchange/latest/userguide/provider-generated-notifications.md): Learn how to create provider-generated notifications.
- [Provider financials on AWS Marketplace](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/data-exchange/latest/userguide/provider-financials.md): Information about the financial aspects of providing data through AWS Data Exchange.


## [Jobs in AWS Data Exchange](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/data-exchange/latest/userguide/jobs.md)

### [Importing assets](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/data-exchange/latest/userguide/importing-assets.md)

You can create an AWS Data Exchange job to import data sets that you want to publish to a product.

- [From an S3 bucket](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/data-exchange/latest/userguide/importing-from-s3.md): When you import assets from Amazon S3 to AWS Data Exchange, the AWS Identity and Access Management (IAM) permissions you use must include the ability to write to the AWS Data Exchange service S3 buckets and to read from the S3 bucket where your assets are stored.
- [From a signed URL](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/data-exchange/latest/userguide/importing-from-url.md): You can use signed URLs to import assets that are not stored in Amazon S3.
- [From an Amazon API Gateway API](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/data-exchange/latest/userguide/import-API-asset.md): AWS Data Exchange subscribers can use their IAM credentials and AWS SDKs to call APIs from data providers.
- [From a datashare for Amazon Redshift](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/data-exchange/latest/userguide/import-RS-asset.md): When you import assets using AWS Data Exchange datashare for Amazon Redshift, you can begin querying, analyzing, and operationalizing third-party Amazon Redshift tables after subscribing.
- [From an AWS Lake Formation (Preview)](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/data-exchange/latest/userguide/import-LF-asset.md): When you import assets from AWS Lake Formation to AWS Data Exchange, the IAM permissions that you use must include the following abilities:

### [Exporting assets](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/data-exchange/latest/userguide/exporting-assets.md)

Both providers and subscribers can export data sets from a published revision of a product in AWS Data Exchange.

### [To an S3 bucket](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/data-exchange/latest/userguide/exporting-from-s3.md)

When you export assets to Amazon S3, the IAM permissions you use must include the ability to read from the AWS Data Exchange service S3 buckets and to write to the S3 bucket where your assets are stored.

- [Using AWS SDKs](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/data-exchange/latest/userguide/export-assets-s3-prog.md): You can use the AWS SDKs to export AWS Data Exchange assets to an S3 bucket using the following instructions.
- [Using the console (Subscriber)](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/data-exchange/latest/userguide/export-asset-s3-console-sub.md): As a subscriber to AWS Data Exchange data products, you can use the AWS Data Exchange console to export AWS Data Exchange assets to an S3 bucket using the following instructions.
- [Using the console (Provider)](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/data-exchange/latest/userguide/export-asset-s3-console-prov.md): As a provider of AWS Data Exchange data products, you can use the AWS Data Exchange console to export AWS Data Exchange assets to an S3 bucket using the following instructions.

### [To a signed URL](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/data-exchange/latest/userguide/Exporting-from-url.md)

You can use signed URLs to export AWS Data Exchange assets that are not stored in Amazon S3.

- [Using AWS SDKs](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/data-exchange/latest/userguide/export-asset-URL-prog.md): You can use the AWS SDKs to export AWS Data Exchange assets to destinations other than S3 buckets.
- [Using the console (Subscriber)](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/data-exchange/latest/userguide/export-asset-url-sub.md): As a subscriber to AWS Data Exchange data products, you can use the AWS Data Exchange console to export AWS Data Exchange assets to destinations other than S3 buckets using the following instructions.
- [Using the console (Provider)](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/data-exchange/latest/userguide/export-asset-URL-console-pro.md): As a provider of AWS Data Exchange data products, you can use the AWS Data Exchange console to export AWS Data Exchange assets to destinations other than S3 buckets using the following instructions.

### [Exporting revisions](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/data-exchange/latest/userguide/exporting-revisions.md)

Both providers and subscribers can export revisions of a data set to an S3 bucket that they have permissions to access.

- [Key patterns when exporting revisions](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/data-exchange/latest/userguide/revision-export-keypatterns.md): When you export an asset revision from AWS Data Exchange, each asset becomes an object in the S3 bucket.
- [Using AWS SDKs](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/data-exchange/latest/userguide/export-rev-s3-prog.md): You can use the AWS SDKs to export AWS Data Exchange asset revisions to an S3 bucket using the following instructions.
- [Using the console (Subscriber)](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/data-exchange/latest/userguide/export-rev-s3-console-sub.md): As a subscriber to AWS Data Exchange data products, you can use the AWS Data Exchange console to export AWS Data Exchange assets to an S3 bucket using the following instructions.
- [Using the console (Provider)](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/data-exchange/latest/userguide/export-rev-s3-console-pro.md): As a provider of AWS Data Exchange data products, you can use the AWS Data Exchange console to export AWS Data Exchange assets to an S3 bucket using the following instructions.
- [Automatically exporting revisions (Subscriber)](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/data-exchange/latest/userguide/auto-export-rev-s3-console-sub.md): When the provider publishes new revisions, you can select to automatically export new revisions to your Amazon S3 bucket.


